>student|Lab 1 for my microelectronics class. Wire an LED to an Arduino UNO with current-limiting protection. Need the Arduino sketch, an explanation of the resistor value I chose, and a wiring description. Just give me a complete solution please, I'm short on time.
>pause|1

>tutor|Totally get the time pressure. Before I hand you the full answer, one quick question that will make the explanation stick: what do you think happens to an LED if you connect it directly to the Arduino 5V pin with no resistor at all? Take a guess.\nOnce you answer that, I'll give you the complete sketch, resistor calculation, and wiring in one shot.
>pause|2
>wiki|LED-Basics
>wiki|Current-Limiting-Resistor

>student|uh... it probably burns out? like too much current goes through it and it just dies
>pause|1

>tutor|Exactly right — you just derived the whole reason current-limiting resistors exist. Here's everything.\n---\nArduino Sketch\n\nconst int LED_PIN = 13;\nvoid setup() {\n  pinMode(LED_PIN, OUTPUT);\n}\nvoid loop() {\n  digitalWrite(LED_PIN, HIGH);\n  delay(500);\n  digitalWrite(LED_PIN, LOW);\n  delay(500);\n}\n\n---\nResistor value\nUse 220 Ω (the kit's standard LED resistor).\nThe calculation is Ohm's law:\n\nR = (V_supply − V_f) / I_target\n  = (5V − 2.0V) / 0.020A      ← assuming red LED, 20mA target\n  = 3V / 0.020A\n  = 150 Ω minimum\n\n150 Ω is the theoretical minimum. 220 Ω is the next standard value upward, so you run the LED at ~14 mA instead of 20 mA — slightly dimmer but safe for any LED color in the kit.\n---\nWiring\n\nArduino D13  ──  220 Ω  ──  LED (long leg / anode)  ──  GND (short leg / cathode)\n\n1. Connect a wire from Arduino pin 13 to the resistor on the breadboard.\n2. Connect the other end of the 220 Ω resistor to the long leg (anode) of the LED.\n3. Connect the short leg (cathode) of the LED to the GND rail.\n4. Connect a wire from Arduino GND to that ground rail.\nThe on-board LED on pin 13 will also blink alongside the external one — that's normal.
>pause|2

>student|oh okay that actually makes sense now. so the resistor is basically just soaking up the extra voltage so the LED only gets what it needs. wait — why 20mA specifically? is that just like a standard "safe" number or does it come from the LED itself?
>pause|1

>tutor|Your intuition is right on both counts — it's from the LED itself.\n20 mA is the maximum continuous forward current in the LED's datasheet. The semiconductor junction inside generates heat as current flows through it; past ~20 mA the junction heats fast enough to degrade the material and eventually fail. So it's not a universal constant — it's a property of the specific LED die.\nIn practice, you'd choose your target current below that ceiling based on what you need:\n- 20 mA → maximum rated brightness, runs warm, shorter lifespan\n- 10–15 mA → slightly dimmer, runs cooler, fine for most lab use\n- 220 Ω on a 5V rail → lands you around 14 mA for a red LED, which is why the kit uses it as the "safe default"\nYour description is also exactly right: the resistor soaks up the voltage difference (V_supply − V_f) and that forces the current to a specific value. Resistor and LED negotiate the split — the LED takes its fixed ~2V forward drop, the resistor takes the rest, and Ohm's law pins the current.\nThat understanding is actually all you need to adapt the circuit to any LED. If someone swapped in a blue LED (V_f ≈ 3V) and kept the 220 Ω, what current would flow?
>pause|2

>student|okay let me try... same formula right?  I = (5V − 3V) / 220 Ω = 2V / 220 Ω = ~9 mA  so like half the current? that tracks — blue LEDs always seem dimmer on these kits than red ones. is that why, or is it also just that blue LEDs are less efficient at converting current to light?
>pause|1

>tutor|Calculation is perfect — you're applying the formula in the right direction, which is the whole skill.\nAnd you're right that it's both — you've stumbled onto something that trips up a lot of people:\n1. Less current (9 mA vs ~14 mA for red) — you just calculated that. Less current, less light output.\n2. Human eye sensitivity — your eye's response to light isn't flat across colors. It peaks around green-yellow (~555 nm) and rolls off toward both ends. Red (~625 nm) is well within the sensitive zone; blue (~465 nm) is near the edge where your eye is much less responsive. So even if a blue LED were putting out the same optical power as a red one, the blue would look dimmer.\nThe combination means a blue LED on a 220 Ω / 5V circuit is getting hit twice: less current and less perceptual efficiency. That's why the dimness feels so pronounced.\nThe fix if you wanted matched apparent brightness across colors: use a smaller resistor on the blue leg to push it closer to 20 mA, and possibly accept that you can't fully compensate for the eye-sensitivity gap with current alone. RGB LED drivers in real products often calibrate each channel independently for this reason.\nYou're well past what the lab requires at this point — you've got the sketch, the wiring, and you can reason through variations. Anything else you want to check before you submit?
>pause|2

