>student|hey actually while I have you — I jumped ahead to lesson 2 and I'm already stuck. I've got an RGB LED and I'm trying to get it to show red. I'm calling analogWrite(redPin, 255) but it's coming out really dim and kind of muddy, not actually red. did I wire something wrong or is there a code issue?
>pause|1

>tutor|That symptom — analogWrite(redPin, 255) giving you dim and muddy instead of bright red — is the #1 RGB LED trap. Quick diagnostic: where is the longest leg of the LED connected?\n- Longest leg → GND: you have a common cathode, and 255 should be full brightness. Something else is off (wrong pin, non-PWM pin, missing resistors).\n- Longest leg → 5V: you have a common anode, and your logic is inverted. 255 is actually off for that color. The "muddy" you're seeing is the other channels picking up stray signal.\nIf it's common anode, the fix is one line — invert the values in code:\n\nanalogWrite(redPin, 255 - brightness);  // 255-255 = 0 = full bright on common anode\n\nOr to just get solid red right now:\n\nanalogWrite(redPin, 0);    // full red\nanalogWrite(greenPin, 255); // off\nanalogWrite(bluePin, 255);  // off\n\nWhere's the long leg wired?
>pause|2

>student|oh — longest leg is going to 5V. so I have the common anode kind? that explains everything. so when I write 255 I'm basically saying "no voltage difference, LED off"?
>pause|1

>tutor|Exactly right. With the common pin at 5V, driving a channel to 5V (255) means zero voltage across that leg — no current, no light. Driving it to 0V (0) gives the full 5V drop across the resistor and LED — maximum brightness. The scale runs backwards from what you'd expect.\nYour mental model is correct: it's not "how bright" you're setting it, it's "how much you're pulling the pin below the common." 255 = no pull = off. 0 = full pull = full bright.\nTry analogWrite(redPin, 0) now and you should get solid red.
>pause|2

>student|yep, that's bright red now. way brighter than before too. okay so the whole scale is just flipped — 0 is full on, 255 is full off. got it. that's going to mess with my head but I understand why.
>pause|1

>tutor|It will mess with your head until you write a helper function — then it disappears:\nvoid setColor(int r, int g, int b) {\n  analogWrite(redPin,   255 - r);\n  analogWrite(greenPin, 255 - g);\n  analogWrite(bluePin,  255 - b);\n}\nNow you call setColor(255, 0, 0) for red and your brain never has to hold the inversion again. Good debug session — you diagnosed it from one symptom and one question.
>pause|2
>wiki|Common-Anode-vs-Common-Cathode

